UNIT encode_value
LANG rust
PRIMARY-SHA256 d91d945d8d17d24001cdc090bc43f8c5eb89a2c7df0116a28a0cb40c0dfc2727
SIG pub fn encode_value(value: &serde_json::Value, ty: &str) -> serde_json::Value
ABI crate shape: a LIBRARY crate ([lib], src/lib.rs exporting pub fn encode_value) depending on serde_json only — no
  main, no I/O, no unsafe. The verifier's generated harness parses each stdin args line, binds value as a
  serde_json::Value and ty as &str, calls encode_value(&value, ty) and prints the result as one JSON line. A throw (R1,
  R2, R5, R6, R7, R10, R13) is a panic; the message is not graded. The result is a Value tree of Object / Array / String / Bool
  only (TABLE NODES); member order is immaterial.
ABI numbers: every json number in value is an IEEE-754 double — read it with as_f64() ONLY, never as_u64 / as_i64 (the
  wire spells 2^64-2048 as 18446744073709550000, which as_u64 reads as a different integer; as_f64 recovers the double
  18446744073709549568, the value the oracle encodes). Integral = finite and x.trunc() == x; the range check and the
  decimal run on the exact value (x as i128, exact below 2^127; i64 needs -2^63 <= x < 2^63, u64 needs 0 <= x < 2^64, -0
  giving "0"). Work on an internal value enum, not on serde_json::Value directly: R5 produces numbers serde cannot hold
  (NaN, the infinities, -0 from a num tag), a distinct INTEGER kind (bigint tag; f64 and json reject it, i64/u64 accept
  it by range — a decimal too long for i128 is out of range), the ABSENT value, and ESCAPED maps (a flag on the map
  node: its "__t" / "__throw" / "__threw" members are data at that node only).
ENVELOPE narrowing: serde_json::Value holds no NaN, infinities or -0 in the argument (the tooling narrows such vectors
  away; the same values built by a num tag ARE in this envelope), no top-level absent value, no byte strings, no keyed
  collections and no unpaired surrogates — so bytes accepts nothing here and the js-extension vectors are outside.
  R13's refuse side is never graded here: the shared oracle's codec refuses an argument past depth 100, so those
  vectors ride the js extension; the shared vectors reach the accept side up to 49 list / 33 map levels (a deeper
  value's result nests past the codec cap and is carried by no oracle). Implement the cap anyway, exactly (depth 0 at the call, + 1 per child value, unchanged through a union alternative, the json position
  and a tag; panic past 100).
MECHANISM RENDER (R12) is ECMA-262 Number::toString. Handle NaN, the infinities and -0 first; 0 renders "0". Take the
  digit COUNT k from format!("{:e}", x.abs()) (Rust's shortest round-trip), then the DIGITS d1..dk and exponent e from
  format!("{:.*e}", k - 1, x.abs()) — the correctly rounded k-digit form, i.e. the closest to x; Rust's shortest form
  alone picks a different last digit when several k-digit strings round-trip (-1928372706752270.2 in JS, ...270.3 from
  {:e}). With n = e + 1, lay out as: k <= n <= 21 -> the digits then n-k zeros; 0 < n <= 21 -> the first n digits, ".",
  the rest; -6 < n <= 0 -> "0." then -n zeros then the digits; otherwise d1, then "." and d2..dk when k > 1, then "e",
  "+" or "-", |n-1|; a "-" prefix for negatives. Never use Display or ryu output directly (Rust prints 1e21 as
  1000000000000000000000 and 1e-7 as 0.0000001). BITS = format!("{:016x}", x.to_bits()), NaN as 7ff8000000000000.
MECHANISM R1 is a hand-written recursive-descent reader over the type text: whitespace is exactly ' ', '\t', '\r', '\n'
  (never str::trim or char::is_whitespace), skipped before every token, the '[' after a keyword included; a NAME is an
  ASCII letter or '_' then ASCII alphanumerics or '_', read greedily; after the TYPE only whitespace may remain. Parse to
  a type enum; R2 compares a canonical key per type (a union's alternative keys sorted, a record's "name:key" field
  strings sorted, other composites in order — never a derived PartialEq, which is order-sensitive), each later
  alternative against every earlier one; COVERS (R2 a-d) is a recursive predicate over the type enum. Count composite
  depth while descending and panic when it reaches 17, checked before descending (the text must never decide the stack
  depth).
MECHANISM R5 num: the four special spellings, else check the json number-literal grammar by hand, then str::parse::<f64>
  (correctly rounded); panic when the result is infinite ("1e999"), when x == 0.0 (either sign) and the mantissa (the
  text before any 'e'/'E') holds a digit '1'..='9' ("1e-400", "-1e-400"), or when it is -0 ("-0.0"). bigint: the canonical decimal grammar by hand ("0" or optional '-' then 1-9 then digits; "-0"
  and leading zeros panic), then i128 (out of range when it does not fit). obj: v must be an Object -> its entries;
  anything else (Array, String, Number, Bool, Null, missing) panics. The new map carries the ESCAPED flag; its entry
  values are ordinary. A marker or tag member counts only when its value is a String; a throw marker only on an Object of exactly one
  entry; serde_json objects have no
  prototype, so every Object is a plain map.
MECHANISM order. R9 compares sort keys as 16-bit code units: a.encode_utf16().cmp(b.encode_utf16()) — NOT str's Ord
  (byte / code-point order puts characters above the surrogate range after astral ones). serde_json::Map iteration
  order is not the input order and does not matter: always sort (a stable sort; keys of a json object are distinct).
  Record fields read the object's own key (get(name)), missing = absent; a record or map position over an Array
  panics. CANON per the primary's R11 and TABLE ESC (equivalently serde_json::to_string of a String, minus its quotes).
DEPENDS-ON serde_json (crate, version 1) — the SIG's value type and the result type; nothing else
PROVENANCE rederive@7b348e351c9f cli/sir/canon.mjs (unchanged since bb8ded84fa50). Language file revised 2026-10-27 (round 5c) with the primary; the ABI
  follows the lab's rust driver (harness generated from the abstract SIG, json crossing as serde_json::Value, a panic as
  a throw).
